# W0 / census B1 — NO END-OF-LINE CONVERSION, EVER, FOR ANYTHING.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE HIGHEST-LEVERAGE PRE-WINDOWS COMMIT: git-for-Windows installs
# with `core.autocrlf=true` by DEFAULT. Without this file, the first Windows
# clone rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout, and this repository's
# core guarantees are byte-exact:
#   * golden PNG/text comparison (goldens/, artifacts/tour-chrome/)
#   * byte-identical re-export and content-addressed state hashes
#   * the contracts/ mirror, which is byte-verified against frozen bogos/spec
# A CRLF rewrite breaks all three at once, noisily but confusingly, and the
# confusion is the expensive part: the failures surface far from the cause.
#
# `-text` marks every path as "do not perform EOL conversion in either
# direction". It does not mark files binary for diff purposes; it only removes
# them from the conversion machinery.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE LANDING (2026-08-24, O-INT): 1438 tracked files, 235 binary
# skipped, 1203 text files scanned — 1183 contain LF and **ZERO contain CRLF**.
# Control: the same scanner reports True on a synthesised CRLF file, so the zero
# is a measurement and not a dead probe. Nothing in the tree needs CRLF
# preserved, which is what makes a blanket `-text` safe here.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS DOES **NOT** PROTECT AGAINST, stated so nobody reads it as total:
# with conversion disabled git also stops NORMALISING on commit. An editor on
# any platform that writes CRLF into a text file will have those bytes stored
# verbatim. That failure is LOUD — it moves a golden or a hash and the suite
# says so — whereas the alternative (`text=auto eol=lf`) would run binary
# heuristics over 235 binary files, and a single misdetection there corrupts a
# golden SILENTLY. Between a loud failure mode and a silent one this repository
# takes the loud one.
#
# Until this file is on the ref a Windows clone uses, that clone MUST set
# `git config core.autocrlf false` BEFORE its first checkout.

* -text

